WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.850
Saving, browser recovery and version history protect
different parts of your work.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:13.270
A portable pumlu document retains application information
that plain Plant UML source does not contain.

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:20.440
Browser recovery helps you return to a workspace, while a
saved file gives you an explicit copy you can manage.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:30.385
We will save and reopen a practice document, inspect
compression and history limits, and review password
protection, recovery, offline use and mobile editing.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:38.175
Choose Save as from File and save a portable practice
document.

00:00:38.295 --> 00:00:42.320
In this recording, the browser uses its download fallback.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:49.890
A browser with native file access may instead let you
choose a writable file and save subsequent edits directly.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:56.335
Check the filename and the app's saved state rather than
assuming that a download updated an older copy.

00:00:56.455 --> 00:01:02.355
Keep one clear working copy and avoid accidentally
continuing in a stale duplicate.

00:01:05.867 --> 00:01:10.567
Save the document, then open that saved file through File
and Open.

00:01:10.687 --> 00:01:14.162
The fallback uses a file selection control.

00:01:14.282 --> 00:01:19.382
Verify the task labels, durations and current preview
after loading it.

00:01:19.502 --> 00:01:24.177
For a connected project, also verify member diagrams and
links.

00:01:24.297 --> 00:01:29.872
Reopening is a useful check before handing over or
deleting a temporary copy.

00:01:29.992 --> 00:01:35.442
A file with the expected name is only useful when it
contains the expected state.

00:01:38.967 --> 00:01:43.842
These screenshots show the actual macOS file picker
workflow in Chrome.

00:01:43.962 --> 00:01:49.437
Choose Save as, select your working folder, enter a clear
file name and press Save.

00:01:49.557 --> 00:01:53.982
Back in the app, check the file name and the Saved to file
indicator.

00:01:54.102 --> 00:02:01.177
To verify the saved copy, choose Open and select the same
portable document in the operating system picker.

00:02:01.297 --> 00:02:08.197
After reopening, we verified that Design still lasts five
days and that the diagram preview is current.

00:02:08.317 --> 00:02:14.367
The file picker appearance and available permissions
depend on your browser and operating system.

00:02:17.867 --> 00:02:20.267
Open Document settings.

00:02:20.387 --> 00:02:27.037
Gzip compression reduces the portable file size; None
keeps the uncompressed representation.

00:02:27.157 --> 00:02:32.007
Inspect Maximum versions and History budget before
changing retention.

00:02:32.127 --> 00:02:37.577
A larger history budget is not a substitute for named
checkpoints and saved copies.

00:02:37.697 --> 00:02:43.572
For this practice, keep the recommended compression and
review the limits without reducing them.

00:02:43.692 --> 00:02:48.592
Apply a setting only when you understand its effect on the
document you will save.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:58.425
Enable Password protection in Document settings to reveal
the password controls and guidance.

00:02:58.545 --> 00:03:01.245
There is no password recovery.

00:03:01.365 --> 00:03:08.765
Use a password you can retain securely, and enter the same
value in confirmation before applying it to a practice
copy.

00:03:08.885 --> 00:03:17.110
Encrypted unsaved changes remain only in the session,
while collaboration and plaintext exports are not end to
end encrypted.

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:25.380
Cancel this inspection to keep the starting file
unprotected; the next exercise creates a separate
protected practice copy.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:33.200
Now apply password protection to a disposable practice
file.

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:40.945
We use an explicitly public training password only for
this example; choose and retain your own password for real
work.

00:03:41.065 --> 00:03:45.940
Enter and confirm it, apply the setting and save the
portable document.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:53.060
Reopen the saved copy, enter the password when prompted
and verify the expected source and current preview.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:58.130
A successful unlock confirms this copy can be recovered
with that password.

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:02.225
Keep the original unencrypted practice file separately.

00:04:05.733 --> 00:04:12.208
Browser recovery can restore recent workspace state, but
it depends on the browser and its stored data.

00:04:12.328 --> 00:04:17.628
Clearing site data or changing browser profiles can remove
that safeguard.

00:04:17.748 --> 00:04:25.448
Inspect the workspace backup and restore commands in File
and keep an explicit backup before a major reorganization.

00:04:25.568 --> 00:04:30.968
A backup, portable document and plain source export serve
different recovery needs.

00:04:31.088 --> 00:04:37.413
Practice restoration in a separate disposable workspace
before relying on it during an incident.

00:04:40.933 --> 00:04:45.858
Create a workspace backup before experimenting with a
disposable plan.

00:04:45.978 --> 00:04:51.678
Change Design from five to seven days so the working copy
differs from that backup.

00:04:51.798 --> 00:04:59.223
Choose Workspace restore and select the saved backup, then
read the browser replacement confirmation before
accepting.

00:04:59.343 --> 00:05:04.268
Restore the practice workspace and verify that Design is
five days again.

00:05:04.388 --> 00:05:11.163
The app downloads a backup of the previous tabs, but
preserve anything useful before accepting replacement.

00:05:11.283 --> 00:05:17.658
This exercise proves the recovery path with a small known
state rather than waiting for an incident.

00:05:21.167 --> 00:05:26.817
When native file access is available, another application
may change the same file.

00:05:26.937 --> 00:05:34.412
Read the external change warning and compare the file with
your current work before choosing a reload or overwrite
path.

00:05:34.532 --> 00:05:39.207
Preserve a separate copy if both versions contain useful
edits.

00:05:39.327 --> 00:05:47.352
Download fallback copies do not behave like a continuously
linked native file, so manage their names and locations
deliberately.

00:05:47.472 --> 00:05:58.372
The earlier saving exercise uses download fallback; a
later controlled file-handle exercise demonstrates the
conflict review without showing the operating system
picker.

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:08.350
Visit and load the application online before expecting an
installed or cached copy to start offline.

00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:15.595
Check the browser's install option where supported, then
test the complete workflow on your device without a
connection.

00:06:15.715 --> 00:06:23.390
Local editing and rendering do not make remote
collaboration, Jira or external renderer links available
offline.

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:26.710
Reconnect before using those services.

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:35.655
The next demonstration uses a production build served
locally to test cached startup, editing, rendering and
saving with its network disabled.

00:06:39.167 --> 00:06:46.242
This demonstration uses a production build served locally,
with its actual service worker and cached assets.

00:06:46.362 --> 00:06:52.887
Wait until caching is ready, then disable this browser
session’s network and reload the application.

00:06:53.007 --> 00:06:56.432
Verify the restored plan and current preview.

00:06:56.552 --> 00:07:02.452
Change Design from five days to six while still offline,
then save a portable copy.

00:07:02.572 --> 00:07:05.047
Finally restore the connection.

00:07:05.167 --> 00:07:15.742
This proves local startup, rendering, editing and saving
for this browser session; remote collaboration, Jira and
external renderer links still require their services.

00:07:19.267 --> 00:07:24.342
These screenshots show Chrome installing the local
training build as a desktop app.

00:07:24.462 --> 00:07:29.737
Check the site identity, choose Next and confirm Install
in the browser prompt.

00:07:29.857 --> 00:07:35.957
The installed app opens in its own window with the saved
practice document and current preview.

00:07:36.077 --> 00:07:43.027
For this controlled update exercise, we served a new
service worker revision with the same application assets.

00:07:43.147 --> 00:07:49.997
When Update available appears, save your work and select
that button to activate the waiting update and reload.

00:07:50.117 --> 00:07:57.367
After the reload, verify the saved file indicator, the
five day Design task and the current diagram preview.

00:07:57.487 --> 00:08:05.912
This verifies the desktop workflow on this Mac; physical
mobile installation remains untested because no device is
available.

00:08:09.433 --> 00:08:17.608
On a narrow screen, use the task view for practical
schedule updates and inspect the selected task carefully
before changing it.

00:08:17.728 --> 00:08:27.053
A compact view helps with labels, progress and supported
task fields, while a larger workspace remains useful for
complex source and relationship review.

00:08:27.173 --> 00:08:32.323
Verify the result and save through the file workflow
supported by the device.

00:08:32.443 --> 00:08:40.018
Test the exact mobile browser you plan to use rather than
assuming its file access is identical to a desktop
browser.

00:08:43.533 --> 00:08:49.108
Open the same practice plan in a separate browser session
at a mobile-sized viewport.

00:08:49.228 --> 00:08:54.403
The mobile task list lets you search and choose work
before opening its properties.

00:08:54.523 --> 00:08:58.773
Select Design and inspect the progress and resource
controls.

00:08:58.893 --> 00:09:04.143
Make a small progress update, apply it and verify the
result in the task list.

00:09:04.263 --> 00:09:09.613
This is a real responsive browser session, not a scaled
desktop screenshot.

00:09:09.733 --> 00:09:17.133
Test file saving and installation on the physical device
you plan to use, because browser capabilities can differ.

00:09:20.633 --> 00:09:27.783
This controlled exercise uses a real browser File System
file handle with a browser-managed training file.

00:09:27.903 --> 00:09:36.803
The operating system picker is not being demonstrated; the
exercise supplies that handle so we can inspect saving and
conflict behavior.

00:09:36.923 --> 00:09:44.123
Save the portable document through the file-handle path,
then modify the stored file outside the diagram editor.

00:09:44.243 --> 00:09:49.893
Attempt Save and inspect the external-file conflict review
before choosing a result.

00:09:50.013 --> 00:09:59.313
For this disposable example, reload the external version
and verify its six day Design duration, then save the
verified result through the handle.

00:09:59.433 --> 00:10:04.658
Test the actual operating system picker separately in your
supported browser.

00:10:08.167 --> 00:10:13.192
Pause and save a practice document, reopen it and verify
its contents.

00:10:13.312 --> 00:10:16.862
Create a named checkpoint and a workspace backup.

00:10:16.982 --> 00:10:20.282
Inspect the compression and retention settings.

00:10:20.402 --> 00:10:26.402
In a separate copy, test password protection and unlock
with a securely retained password.

00:10:26.522 --> 00:10:32.472
Finally test offline startup and mobile saving on the
devices you will actually use.

00:10:32.592 --> 00:10:37.992
Record which checks passed and keep a known good portable
copy before continuing.
